      "Businesses pay employees, suppliers and service providers by cash, cheque, transfer, standing order, direct debit and point-of-sale methods. A standing order is normally an instruction to pay a set amount regularly; a direct debit authorises the recipient to collect under agreed terms. Choose a method that suits security, timing, records and the recipient's access. Keep authorisation and proof of payment.",
      "Payroll starts with employee records, agreed rates, time cards, time books or electronic attendance. Check ordinary hours, overtime, leave and approved deductions. Payroll software and spreadsheets can calculate earnings, deductions and reports, but records must be checked for incorrect rates, duplicated employees and unauthorised changes. Separate preparation, authorisation and payment where possible.",
      "Statutory deductions are required under applicable law, such as income tax or social security. Voluntary deductions are authorised by the employee, such as a savings contribution or loan repayment. Rates and thresholds vary by territory and date; an exercise should supply them or use verified current rules. In this course's calculators, entered deductions are exercise values and are not asserted to be current legal tax calculations.",
